/* =====================================================================
   Conclusion Lab question bank (Year 13, 7132).
   Every question is a real AQA question. For each one the student gets
   two finished paragraphs (one for, one against) and writes only the
   conclusion.
   Sources for each entry: the AQA mark scheme (indicative content and
   evaluation) and the examiner report for that series. Figures in the
   paragraphs have been worked out from the original case material.
   Units 3.9 and 3.10 are not used. No models removed from the spec.
   ===================================================================== */
(function(){
"use strict";

/* what each kind of question needs from a conclusion */
const TYPES={
  p1:{name:'Paper 1 essay',marks:25,levels:5,
    tip:'Paper 1 has no case study. Answer the exact question, decide which side carries more weight and why, and say what the answer depends on. If the question uses a word like always, guaranteed, inevitable or all, deal with that word directly.',
    marker:'Paper 1 25-mark essay (Levels 1–5). No case study. A top conclusion answers the exact question, weighs the two arguments (says which carries more weight and in what circumstances, with a reason), explains what the answer depends on in terms specific to this question, deals with any absolute word in the question (always, guaranteed, inevitable, all), and ends with a clear final judgement. It may use the businesses or industries from the paragraphs to show when the answer changes.'},
  p2o:{name:'Paper 2 essay',marks:16,levels:4,
    tip:'This question is about all businesses, not the one in the case. Use the two different industries in the paragraphs to show when the answer is yes and when it is no, then say what decides it.',
    marker:'Paper 2 16-mark "all businesses" essay (Levels 1–4). A top conclusion answers the exact question, uses the two contrasting industries or businesses from the paragraphs to show where the statement holds and where it does not, explains the deciding condition with a reason, may add a second condition, and ends with a clear judgement in the words of the question. A conclusion that only talks about one business, or says "it depends on the business" without saying what about the business, is not top level.'},
  p2d:{name:'Paper 2 data question',marks:16,levels:4,
    tip:'Your judgement has to come from the data. Pick the figures that decide it and explain why they matter more than the figures on the other side. If the question asks you to recommend or advise, make a clear decision and justify it.',
    marker:'Paper 2 16-mark question based on the case data (Levels 1–4). A top conclusion makes a clear decision on the exact question, uses the decisive figures from the data (calculated, not just copied) to explain why one side outweighs the other, says what the decision depends on in this case, and stays on the question asked. The examiner reports criticise conclusions that list both sides and then just say "I recommend" without saying why that is the right move.'},
  p3o:{name:'Paper 3 essay',marks:24,levels:5,
    tip:'Judge the statement for all businesses. Use the case business and the business from outside the case to show where the statement holds and where it does not, then say what decides it.',
    marker:'Paper 3 24-mark question about all or any businesses (Levels 1–5). A top conclusion answers the exact question for businesses in general, contrasts the case business with the business from outside the case, explains what decides the answer with a reason, and ends with a clear judgement. A conclusion that only talks about the case business cannot reach Level 5.'},
  p3d:{name:'Paper 3 data question',marks:20,levels:5,
    tip:'Answer the exact question about the case business. Weigh how big the evidence on each side is, using the figures, and keep your judgement in proportion — don\'t claim the business will collapse unless the data shows it.',
    marker:'Paper 3 20-mark question about the case business (Levels 1–5). A top conclusion answers the precise question (not a different one, such as whether to go ahead with a plan when the question is about risk), weighs the size of the evidence on each side using the figures, explains what the judgement depends on in this business, and is measured rather than exaggerated. Examiners praised students who did not overstate the problem.'}
};

const Q=[
/* ============================ PAPER 1, 25 MARKS ============================ */
{id:'P1-2017-23',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2017, Q23',
 q:'A business has recently started to use Elkington\'s Triple Bottom Line to assess its overall performance. To what extent do you think the business is likely to have lower profits as a result of this?',
 forP:`On one hand, using the Triple Bottom Line is likely to lower profits because it pushes the business to spend more on people and the planet. This can be seen in budget clothing retailing. If a retailer starts measuring how factory workers are treated and how much its products damage the environment, it is likely to pay suppliers more for safer factories and switch to more sustainable materials such as organic cotton. This raises its cost of sales. Because budget clothing shoppers buy mainly on price, demand is price elastic, so the retailer cannot pass the higher costs on without losing a large share of its sales to cheaper rivals. As a result, it has to absorb the costs, so its gross profit margin falls. These costs also have to be paid straight away, whereas any benefit from a better reputation takes years to build. This shows that using the Triple Bottom Line is likely to lead to lower profits, at least in the short term, for a business whose customers are price sensitive.`,
 againstP:`However, using the Triple Bottom Line will not necessarily lower profits, because the people and planet measures can increase demand and reduce some costs. This can be seen at Patagonia, the outdoor clothing business, which uses recycled materials and repairs customers' old products. Its customers value this, so it acts as a point of differentiation (Porter). This means demand is more price inelastic, so Patagonia can charge premium prices without losing many sales, and the extra cost of sustainable materials is more than covered. Customers who share its values are also more brand loyal, so it needs to spend less on promotion to keep them. In addition, the planet measures often lead to less waste and lower energy use, which reduces unit costs over time. This shows that the Triple Bottom Line does not have to lower profits, and can even raise them, when customers value social and environmental performance.`,
 depends:['what managers actually change as a result: the Triple Bottom Line is a way of measuring performance, so profit only changes if the results lead to different decisions','how price sensitive the customers are and whether they value people and planet (can extra costs be passed on?)','the time period: costs are immediate, savings and loyalty build up','how well the business already performs on the people and planet measures (little may need to change)','the business may accept lower profit as a price worth paying'],
 report:'The mark scheme says evaluation may consider how well the business is already doing on the three measures, what actions managers take as a result and their effect on demand and costs, and that the business may not mind a fall in profit.',
 model:`Overall, the business is likely to have lower profits in the short term, but not necessarily in the long term, and it depends mainly on how price sensitive its customers are. The Triple Bottom Line only measures performance, so profit changes only if managers act on the results. For a budget clothing retailer, customers buy on price, so the extra cost of safer factories and better materials cannot be passed on and margins fall. For a business like Patagonia, customers value the people and planet side, so demand is more price inelastic and higher prices cover the extra costs. It also depends on the time period, because the costs are paid straight away while the savings from less waste and stronger loyalty build up over several years. Therefore, the business is only likely to have lower profits as a result if its customers buy mainly on price, and even then the fall may be temporary.`},

{id:'P1-2018-23',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2018, Q23',
 q:'To what extent do you think that UK businesses will experience a fall in profit if governments limit free trade by adopting more protectionist policies?',
 forP:`On one hand, protectionism is likely to reduce the profits of UK businesses that export. This can be illustrated by Scotch whisky. In 2019 the USA put a 25% tariff on single malt Scotch whisky imported from the UK. This meant US importers had to pay more, so the price of Scotch in US shops rose. Because there are close substitutes, such as American bourbon and Irish whiskey, demand for Scotch is fairly price elastic, so a higher price led to a bigger percentage fall in sales volume. Distillers could have cut their own prices to keep the shop price the same, but that would have squeezed their profit margins instead. Either way, the tariff reduced the profit they made on sales to one of their biggest export markets. This shows that UK businesses which depend on exports are likely to experience a fall in profit when other governments adopt protectionist policies.`,
 againstP:`However, protectionism does not cause a fall in profit for all UK businesses, because some are protected from overseas competition. This can be seen in the UK steel industry, where the UK government has kept quotas and tariffs on imported steel. These limit how much cheap foreign steel can come into the UK. As a result, UK buyers have fewer low-priced alternatives, which reduces the threat of substitutes and the rivalry from overseas producers (Porter's Five Forces). UK steel producers can therefore sell a larger volume in their home market, or hold their prices higher than they could if they faced cheap imports, which increases their revenue. Since many of their costs, such as the blast furnaces, are fixed, the extra output lowers unit costs, so profit margins improve. This shows that protectionism can increase the profits of UK businesses that compete against imports in the UK market.`,
 depends:['whether the business exports, imports materials, or competes with imports in the UK','which measures are used, against which goods and how long they last','how price elastic demand is for the exporter\'s product','whether the business can find other markets or adapt its prices','exchange rate changes and whether the business has prepared'],
 report:'The mark scheme says the extent depends on whether firms are exporters, importers or purely domestic, what measures are introduced and for how long, exchange rates, and how far businesses have prepared.',
 model:`Overall, UK businesses will not all experience a fall in profit, because the effect depends mainly on whether a business sells abroad or competes against imports in the UK. For an exporter such as a Scotch whisky distiller, a tariff makes its product dearer than close substitutes, so sales and margins fall together and profit is hit hard. For a UK steel producer, the same type of policy keeps cheap imports out, so it can sell more at better prices and its profit is likely to rise. The fall in profit is also bigger the more price elastic the exporter's product is, since that decides how much sales drop when the price rises. It also depends on how long the policies last, because a short-term tariff only causes a temporary fall while the business finds other markets. Therefore, protectionism is likely to reduce the profits of UK exporters, but it can increase the profits of businesses that compete with imports at home.`},

{id:'P1-2019-22',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2019, Q22',
 q:'A competitive advantage can be achieved through low-cost or through differentiation. Which of these types of competitive advantage is it easier for a UK business to maintain? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, differentiation is easier for a UK business to maintain because it can be protected in ways that low cost cannot. This can be illustrated by Dyson, which spends heavily on research and development and holds thousands of patents on its motors and designs. Patents stop rivals legally copying the technology for up to 20 years, which acts as a barrier to entry (Porter's Five Forces). This means Dyson's products stay different, so customers see fewer close substitutes and demand is more price inelastic, allowing it to keep charging premium prices. At the same time, a UK business finds it hard to compete on cost because UK wages and property costs are higher than in many other countries, so an overseas rival can often produce more cheaply. This shows that differentiation, especially when it is backed by patents and a strong brand, is easier for a UK business to maintain than low cost.`,
 againstP:`However, low cost can be easier to maintain when it is based on a scale that rivals cannot match. This can be seen at Primark, owned by the UK company Associated British Foods. Primark buys clothing in such huge volumes that it gets large purchasing economies of scale, and it spends very little on advertising. As a result its unit costs are lower than most clothing retailers, so it can charge very low prices and still make a profit. A new competitor would need similar volumes to get similar costs, which is very difficult, so its cost advantage is hard to copy. In contrast, differentiation can disappear. When Dyson's original patents ran out, rivals such as Shark brought out similar bagless cleaners at lower prices, so Dyson had to keep spending on new products to stay ahead. This shows that low cost based on scale can be easier to maintain than differentiation, which needs constant investment.`,
 depends:['what the advantage is based on and whether rivals can copy it (patents, brand, scale)','whether the UK business competes with overseas producers who have lower costs','how quickly technology and tastes change in the market','how much the business can keep investing in innovation','barriers to entry and the stage of the product life cycle'],
 report:'The mark scheme says the relative ease depends on the economy the business competes from, barriers to entry, growth prospects and current advantage, and notes UK businesses tend to compete internationally through differentiation. The examiner report rewarded use of Porter\'s generic strategies and real businesses.',
 model:`Overall, differentiation is easier for most UK businesses to maintain, but it depends mainly on whether the advantage can be copied. A UK business usually starts with higher wages and property costs than overseas rivals, so a cost advantage is only safe when it rests on a scale few others can reach, as Primark's buying power does. Differentiation is protected for longer when it is backed by patents and a brand, which is why Dyson can keep charging premium prices. However, Dyson's experience after its first patents ran out shows that differentiation is only maintained if the business keeps investing in new products, so it also depends on how much it can afford to spend on research and development. Therefore, differentiation is easier for a UK business to maintain as long as it keeps innovating, while low cost is only easier for the few UK businesses big enough to have economies of scale that rivals cannot match.`},

{id:'P1-2020N-21',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, November 2020, Q21',
 q:'\'Limited liability benefits all stakeholders of a public limited company.\' Do you agree? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, limited liability benefits stakeholders because it makes it possible for a plc to raise large amounts of finance. Shareholders know that the most they can lose is the money they paid for their shares, so they are willing to invest in businesses they do not run. This allows a plc such as Ocado to raise hundreds of millions of pounds by issuing new shares to build its automated warehouses. That investment creates jobs for employees, larger orders for suppliers of equipment and better service for customers. Without limited liability, few people would invest in a business they could not control, because they could lose their homes and savings if it failed. As a result, businesses would stay smaller and would grow more slowly. This shows that limited liability benefits not only shareholders but also employees, suppliers and customers, because it allows a plc to grow.`,
 againstP:`However, limited liability does not benefit all stakeholders, because it moves the risk of failure onto the people the company owes money to. This can be seen when Carillion plc collapsed in 2018. Its shareholders lost the value of their shares, but they could not be asked to pay any more. In contrast, thousands of suppliers and subcontractors were owed money that they were never fully paid. Small suppliers that had given Carillion generous credit terms were hit hardest, as they had already paid for materials and labour, so some of them had cash flow problems of their own. Employees also lost their jobs. Banks were in a better position than suppliers if their loans were secured on assets. This shows that limited liability benefits shareholders, but it can leave suppliers and other creditors worse off when a plc fails.`,
 depends:['whether the plc is likely to fail: limited liability only matters to creditors in liquidation, so in good times nearly everyone benefits','how exposed each stakeholder is (unsecured suppliers who gave credit versus secured lenders and regularly paid employees)','the benefits of growth and risk-taking that limited liability allows'],
 report:'The mark scheme says it is clearly advantageous for shareholders, but for other stakeholders it raises the risk of not being paid, balanced against the opportunities it creates, and that it matters much less in a boom because it only applies when a business goes into liquidation.',
 model:`I disagree that limited liability benefits all stakeholders, because it protects shareholders by moving the risk of failure onto anyone the company owes money to. While a plc is successful, almost every stakeholder benefits, since limited liability is what lets a business like Ocado raise large sums of share capital that create jobs and orders. But when a plc fails, as Carillion did, the shareholders' losses stop at their shares while unsecured suppliers lose money they are owed. So the answer depends mainly on whether the plc is likely to fail. It also depends on how exposed each stakeholder is: a bank with security is protected, but a small supplier that gave long credit terms is not. Therefore, limited liability always benefits shareholders and benefits most stakeholders most of the time, but it does not benefit all stakeholders.`},

{id:'P1-2022-22',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2022, Q22',
 q:'To what extent might the use of the Boston Matrix enable a multi-product business to achieve overall sales growth?',
 forP:`On one hand, the Boston Matrix can help a multi-product business achieve sales growth because it shows where to invest. It puts each product into a category based on its market share and the growth of its market. A business such as Coca-Cola can see that its original Coca-Cola is a cash cow, with a high share of a mature market, so it generates far more cash than it needs. Managers can move that cash into products in fast-growing markets, such as problem children like a new low-sugar drink, and spend it on promotion and wider distribution. Because the market is growing, there are lots of new customers, so the extra spending can increase sales without having to take them from rivals. If the product gains market share, it becomes a star. This shows that the Boston Matrix can enable overall sales growth by helping the business put its money behind the products with the most growth potential.`,
 againstP:`However, the Boston Matrix only shows the position of products now; it does not create sales growth itself. Deciding to back a problem child does not mean customers will buy it. This can be seen at Google, which put large amounts of money into products such as Google+, its social network, but closed it in 2019 because users did not switch from rivals. The matrix also uses market growth and market share, so it says nothing about whether a product is profitable or about what customers want. In addition, if the economy goes into recession, growth in most markets slows down, so a product that looked like a star may stop growing whatever the business does. As a result, the sales growth depends on the decisions taken after the analysis, such as the product and its marketing mix, rather than on the matrix itself. This shows that the Boston Matrix is unlikely to enable sales growth on its own.`,
 depends:['how well the business acts on the analysis (product, promotion, marketing mix)','whether it has cash cows strong enough to fund the investment','economic conditions: in a recession market growth slows','the matrix is a snapshot based on share and growth, not profit'],
 report:'The mark scheme says evaluation should assess the usefulness and limitations of the Boston Matrix for sales growth. Answers that argue other factors are more important without discussing the matrix are not answering the question.',
 model:`Overall, the Boston Matrix can help a multi-product business achieve sales growth, but only to a limited extent, because it shows managers where to invest rather than creating the growth itself. It is most useful when the business has strong cash cows, as Coca-Cola does, because the cash can be moved into products in fast-growing markets where extra sales are easiest to win. But, as Google found with Google+, backing a problem child does not mean customers will buy it, so how much the matrix helps depends mainly on how good the marketing decisions are after the analysis. It also depends on the economy, since in a recession market growth slows, so even the right portfolio decisions may only protect sales rather than grow them. Therefore, the Boston Matrix is a useful starting point for sales growth, but it only achieves it when it is backed by enough cash and effective marketing.`},

{id:'P1-2023-24',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2023, Q24',
 q:'A business decides to make increasing profits its main objective. Is it inevitable that this business will need to improve its quality to achieve this objective? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, improving quality is often necessary to increase profits. For a business using a differentiation strategy (Porter), quality is what makes customers willing to pay more. If it improves the quality of its products, customers see more value in them, so demand becomes more price inelastic and the business can raise its price without losing many sales, widening the gap between price and unit cost. Improving the quality of the process can also cut costs. This can be illustrated by Toyota, whose focus on quality, with every worker able to stop the line when they spot a fault, means far fewer defects. Fewer faulty cars means less waste, less reworking and fewer warranty claims, so unit costs fall. As a result, profit margins rise from both sides. This shows that improving quality can be the key way for a business to achieve its objective of increasing profits.`,
 againstP:`However, it is not inevitable that a business needs to improve its quality, as profits can be increased in other ways. This can be seen at Ryanair, which competes through cost leadership. Its customers choose it because it is cheap, not because of the quality of the service, so improving quality would add costs without bringing in many extra customers. Instead, Ryanair has raised its profits by keeping unit costs low, for example by flying one type of aircraft and using cheaper airports, and by adding extra charges for bags and seat choice. These charges raise revenue per passenger without improving quality at all. Profits can also rise because of things outside the business, such as falling fuel prices or a growing economy. This shows that a business can increase its profits without improving its quality, especially if it competes on price.`,
 depends:['the competitive strategy: differentiation versus low cost','product quality versus process quality (process quality can cut costs for any business)','the nature of the market and what customers value','other ways to raise profit: cutting costs, extra revenue, external factors'],
 report:'The mark scheme concludes that quality can be an important influence on profitability but it is not inevitable that improved quality is needed, because profit can be improved by lower costs, image building, barriers to entry and external factors.',
 model:`Overall, it is not inevitable that the business will need to improve its quality, because it depends mainly on how the business competes. For a business using differentiation, quality is what allows it to charge higher prices, so improving it is close to essential if profits are to rise. For a low-cost business like Ryanair, customers choose on price, so profits can rise through lower costs and extra charges with no improvement in quality. The one exception is the quality of the process, since cutting defects as Toyota has done lowers unit costs for any business, so most businesses would gain from it even if they do not strictly need it. Therefore, improving quality is often helpful but not inevitable: it is only necessary when the business competes on the quality of what it sells rather than on price.`},

{id:'P1-2024-22',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2024, Q22',
 q:'A business changes its human resource management (HRM) approach from hard HRM to soft HRM. To what extent is this change guaranteed to increase the motivation of all employees?',
 forP:`On one hand, moving to soft HRM is likely to increase motivation because it treats employees as an asset to be developed rather than a cost to be controlled. Employees are given more training, more say in decisions and more responsibility. This can be seen at the John Lewis Partnership, where staff are partners who are consulted on how the business is run. According to Herzberg, responsibility, recognition and achievement are motivators because they come from the job itself, so giving employees more of them increases their motivation. Maslow would say this meets esteem and self-actualisation needs. As a result, employees are more willing to put in extra effort and suggest improvements, so productivity rises and labour turnover falls. This shows that a change from hard to soft HRM can increase the motivation of employees who want responsibility and development.`,
 againstP:`However, the change is not guaranteed to motivate all employees, because not everyone wants or values more responsibility. For example, in a large distribution warehouse many staff are on short-term contracts, low hourly pay and do repetitive tasks such as picking orders. According to Maslow, if their physiological and safety needs, such as a secure income, are not met, more involvement in decisions will not motivate them, because those needs come first. Herzberg would also say that if pay and job security, which are hygiene factors, are poor, employees will be dissatisfied whatever else changes. In addition, managers who are used to giving orders may resent losing control, and some employees may see the change as more work for the same pay. As a result, motivation may stay the same or even fall for some groups. This shows that soft HRM does not guarantee higher motivation for all employees.`,
 depends:['the type of work and skill level of employees','where employees are on Maslow\'s hierarchy and whether hygiene factors such as pay and security are adequate','how well the change is introduced and whether managers support it','the culture of the business'],
 report:'The mark scheme says the effect depends on the nature of the workforce and their roles, individual attitudes (autocratic managers may resent losing control), the nature of the task (repetitive work may see no difference) and the culture of the business. Do not use models removed from the specification.',
 model:`Overall, the change is not guaranteed to increase the motivation of all employees, because it depends mainly on the type of work employees do and which needs they are trying to meet. For skilled employees, such as the partners at John Lewis, soft HRM gives them the responsibility and recognition that Herzberg sees as real motivators, so their motivation is likely to rise. For low-skilled warehouse workers on insecure pay, those higher needs are not yet their priority, so more involvement may make little difference. It also depends on whether the business keeps pay and job security at a fair level during the change, because without those hygiene factors more empowerment will not motivate anyone. Therefore, moving to soft HRM is likely to increase the motivation of many employees, but it is not guaranteed for all of them.`},

{id:'P1-2024-24',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2024, Q24',
 q:'Will the introduction of lean production always lead to lower unit costs? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, lean production usually lowers unit costs because it removes waste. With just-in-time, materials arrive only when they are needed, so the business holds much less inventory. This means it needs less warehouse space, ties up less cash in stock and loses less to damage or items going out of date. This can be illustrated by Toyota, which developed lean production and also uses kaizen, where workers suggest many small improvements to how jobs are done. Over time these improvements increase labour productivity, so more is produced with the same workforce. As fixed and labour costs are spread over more output, unit costs fall. Lean production also focuses on getting things right first time, which reduces the cost of reworking faulty products. This shows that the introduction of lean production can lead to lower unit costs by cutting waste and raising productivity.`,
 againstP:`However, lean production will not always lead to lower unit costs, especially if supplies are disrupted. In 2021 a global shortage of computer chips forced car manufacturers such as Jaguar Land Rover to stop production at UK factories. Because they held very little buffer stock, production halted soon after deliveries stopped. While the lines were idle, fixed costs such as factory rent and salaried staff still had to be paid, but far fewer cars were made, so unit costs rose sharply. In addition, buying in small quantities means losing bulk-buying discounts, and introducing lean takes time and money, as staff need training and managers need to change the culture. In the first year or two these costs can push unit costs up before any savings arrive. This shows that lean production does not always lead to lower unit costs, particularly when suppliers are unreliable or in the early stages.`,
 depends:['the reliability of suppliers and the supply chain','the time period: short-term implementation costs versus long-term savings','how well it is implemented and whether the culture changes','whether the business loses bulk-buying discounts','whether the business uses lean to improve quality rather than to cut cost'],
 report:'The mark scheme says lean should lead to lower unit costs in many situations, but may raise them if implementation is inefficient or the emphasis is on higher quality, and that changes are likely to raise unit costs in the short term even if they fall in the medium or long term.',
 model:`Overall, lean production will not always lead to lower unit costs, although it usually does in the long run. The savings come from holding less inventory and from continuous improvement, which is why Toyota has been able to keep its unit costs low. But lean depends heavily on reliable suppliers, and when the chip shortage stopped deliveries, car factories with no buffer stock had to halt while their fixed costs carried on, so unit costs rose sharply. The answer therefore depends mainly on how reliable the business's suppliers are. It also depends on how long the business has been using lean, because training and changing the culture add costs at first before the savings come through. Therefore, lean production lowers unit costs for a business with dependable suppliers once it is properly established, but not always, and not straight away.`},

{id:'P1-2025-22',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2025, Q22',
 q:'The marketing department of a business wishes to increase its market share in a growing market. To what extent is market research the most important activity of the marketing department in order to achieve this objective?',
 forP:`On one hand, market research is the most important activity because, in a growing market, many of the customers are new and the business does not yet know what they want. This can be seen in the electric car market, where many buyers are first-time owners worried about how far the car can travel on one charge and where they can charge it. Primary research, such as surveys and focus groups, tells the business which of these concerns matters most and which segment of buyers to target. As a result, the business can design the product, set the price and choose the promotion around what these customers actually want, so it does not waste money on features they do not value. This makes its product more attractive than rivals' to the new customers entering the market, so its market share rises. This shows that market research is the most important activity, because it guides every other decision the marketing department makes.`,
 againstP:`However, market research does not win customers on its own; it is the marketing mix that actually persuades them to buy. This can be seen in the energy and sports drinks market, where the Prime brand grew very quickly in 2023 mainly through promotion on social media by its well-known founders, who already had millions of followers. Its success came from promotion and from getting the drink into shops, not from detailed research. In a growing market, competitors are also entering, so the business that gets its product onto shelves and in front of customers first is likely to gain share. Market research also costs money and takes time, and if the sample is small its findings may be unreliable. As a result, the activities that make the most direct difference to market share are promotion, distribution and pricing. This shows that market research is not always the most important activity of the marketing department.`,
 depends:['how well the research is used to shape the rest of the marketing mix','how much the business already knows about its customers','how fast the market is growing and how quickly competitors are entering','the budget available and how reliable the research is'],
 report:'The mark scheme says market research generally affects growth by improving the quality of other marketing activities, so its importance relates to how well it is used. Answers that just assert another activity is more important, without analysing market research, are limited to Level 2.',
 model:`Overall, market research is important but it is not the most important activity in every case, because it only raises market share if it shapes the other marketing decisions. In a growing market where customers are new and their needs are unclear, as with first-time electric car buyers, research is the starting point that stops the business wasting money on the wrong product. But in a market like energy drinks, where Prime grew mainly through promotion on social media, the activities that actually win customers are promotion and distribution, and research only helps if it leads to better versions of these. So the answer depends mainly on how much the business already knows about its customers: the less it knows, the more important research becomes. Therefore, market research is the most important activity at the start, when it decides the rest of the marketing mix, but after that the mix itself does more to increase market share.`},

{id:'P1-2026-23',type:'p1',src:'Paper 1, June 2026, Q23',
 q:'\'Capital-intensive processes are always more profitable than labour-intensive processes.\' Do you agree? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, capital-intensive processes can be more profitable because they lower unit costs when output is high. This can be illustrated by car manufacturing, such as Nissan's factory in Sunderland, where robots carry out most of the welding and painting. Robots can work around the clock without breaks and produce the same quality every time, so labour productivity is much higher and there are fewer defects. Although the machinery has a very high fixed cost, the factory produces hundreds of thousands of cars a year, so the fixed cost is spread over a huge output and the cost per car is low. As a result, the business can sell at a competitive price and still make a good profit margin on each car. This shows that a capital-intensive process can be more profitable than a labour-intensive one when a business makes large volumes of a standardised product.`,
 againstP:`However, capital-intensive processes are not always more profitable, because they need high and steady sales to cover their fixed costs. A labour-intensive process can be more profitable where customers value products made by hand. This can be seen with the bespoke tailors on Savile Row in London, where each suit is cut and sewn to fit one customer. Output is small, so expensive machinery would never be paid back. More importantly, customers pay thousands of pounds for a suit precisely because it is handmade, so demand is price inelastic and the profit margin on each suit is high. Labour-intensive businesses are also more flexible, because if demand falls they can cut hours rather than being stuck with machinery that still has to be paid for. In contrast, a capital-intensive business has a high break-even output, so a fall in sales can quickly turn a profit into a loss. This shows that labour-intensive processes can be more profitable for low-volume, customised products.`,
 depends:['the volume of output and how stable demand is','whether customers value a handmade or personal product (premium prices)','the time period: high start-up cost of machinery versus long-run savings','labour costs and the availability of skilled staff'],
 report:'The examiner report noted strong real-world examples for capital-intensive processes but a weak range for labour-intensive ones, and rewarded judgements that brought in context, such as the high start-up cost of capital versus lower unit costs in the long run. The word "always" should be challenged.',
 model:`Overall, I disagree that capital-intensive processes are always more profitable, because it depends mainly on the volume a business sells and how stable that demand is. For a car manufacturer like Nissan producing hundreds of thousands of similar cars, the high fixed cost of robots is spread over a huge output, so its unit costs are far below what a labour-intensive process could achieve. For a bespoke tailor, output is small and customers pay a premium because each suit is handmade, so the labour-intensive process is clearly more profitable. It also depends on the time period, as the heavy start-up cost of machinery can mean lower profit for several years before the savings on labour pay it back. Therefore, capital-intensive processes are usually more profitable for high-volume, standardised products, but not always, and not for businesses selling customised products.`},

/* ============================ PAPER 2, DATA QUESTIONS (16) ============================ */
{id:'P2-2020N-01.4',type:'p2d',src:'Paper 2, November 2020, Q01.4',
 q:'Using the data, recommend whether Drake Hotels PLC should go ahead with the proposed investment. Justify your answer.',
 facts:['Drake Hotels PLC owns 20 budget hotels in the UK, in a very competitive industry. Operating profit 2019–20: £18 million.','New Chief Executive Mary Myers (previously Marketing Manager of an upmarket hotel chain) was appointed to increase profitability. She proposes a luxury hotel in Oxford targeting overseas visitors.','Appendix A: average rate of return 12% per year; net present value £25 million (first eight years). Source: Chief Executive.','Appendix B: 20% probability the pound is 10% higher than forecast; 30% probability overseas GDP growth is 1.5% higher than forecast.','Appendix C: current assets £15m, current liabilities £18m, total equity £170m, non-current liabilities £280m.','Appendix D: initial investment £20m; income elasticity of demand +2; 70% of customers from overseas; cost of borrowing 8% a year.'],
 forP:`On one hand, Drake Hotels should go ahead with the investment because the returns are strong. The ARR of 12% is 4 percentage points higher than the 8% cost of borrowing, so even if the whole £20m is borrowed, the hotel should earn more than the interest. It is also three times Drake's current ROCE of 4% (£18m ÷ £450m capital employed), so the project would raise the profitability of the business, which is exactly what Mary was appointed to do. The NPV of £25m is positive, which means the discounted returns are worth £25m more than the £20m it costs. The sensitivity analysis is also more likely to help than hurt. There is a 30% chance that overseas GDP growth is 1.5% higher than forecast, and with an income elasticity of +2 this would increase demand by about 3%. As 70% of customers come from overseas, this would raise bookings further. This shows that Drake Hotels should go ahead because the investment is likely to increase its profitability.`,
 againstP:`However, Drake Hotels should be careful about going ahead because of its financial position and the risks. Its gearing is already high at 62.2% (£280m ÷ £450m), and if it borrows the extra £20m this rises to about 63.8%, so more of its profit will go on interest payments. Its current ratio is only 0.83 (£15m ÷ £18m), so it does not have spare cash to fund the hotel itself. There is also a 20% chance that the pound is 10% higher than forecast. This would make holidays in the UK more expensive for overseas visitors, and since 70% of the hotel's customers are from overseas, demand and the returns in Appendix A could fall. In addition, moving from budget hotels into luxury is a big change for the brand, and all the figures come from Mary herself, who has a reason to make the project look good. This shows that the investment carries real financial risk and may not deliver the forecast returns.`,
 depends:['how the £20m is financed given gearing of 62.2% and a current ratio of 0.83','whether Mary\'s forecasts are realistic, as she produced them','the balance of risk: 30% chance of higher overseas growth versus 20% chance of a stronger pound','whether Drake can manage a luxury brand alongside budget hotels'],
 report:'The examiner report says too many students gave reasons for and against and then just said "I recommend". Justify requires the response to say why the recommendation is the right move. Better students used their earlier calculations, linked data together and weighed it to make a justified recommendation.',
 model:`Overall, I recommend that Drake Hotels goes ahead with the investment, because the returns are large enough to outweigh the financial risks. The most important figure is the ARR of 12%, which is well above the 8% cost of borrowing and three times the current ROCE of 4%, so even if the hotel is financed entirely by debt it should more than cover the interest and raise the profitability of the whole business. The high gearing is a concern, but the extra £20m only takes it from 62.2% to about 63.8%, which is a small change for a project with an NPV of £25m. The exchange rate risk is real, but it is less likely to happen (20%) than the boost from higher overseas growth (30%). However, the decision depends on whether Mary's figures are accurate, as she produced them herself. Therefore, Drake should go ahead, as long as the board checks her forecasts before committing the money.`},

{id:'P2-2022-01.5',type:'p2d',src:'Paper 2, June 2022, Q01.5',
 q:'To what extent does the information provided suggest that now is a good time to launch a new brand of tea?',
 facts:['Tea drinking in the UK is declining as younger customers choose coffee. Extrapolation suggests coffee will outsell tea by 2025.','A "health tea" niche (fruit, herbal and green teas) is growing, helped by its link with wellbeing.','Appendix A: UK black tea sales fell from about 96 million kg (2012) to about 68 million kg (2020).','Appendix B (2019 sales): Twinings £108m (+3.8%), PG Tips £99m (−7.2%), Taylors £98m (+5.0%), Tetley £90m (+0.4%), Pukka £23m (+15.4%, organic and ethical), other brands £144m.','Appendix C: price elasticity of demand: black tea −1.2, health teas −0.5.','Appendix D: 28% of 18–24-year-olds never drink black tea (16% of over-65s). 32% of women and 19% of men drink health tea. 62% say ethical sourcing is important.'],
 forP:`On one hand, now could be a good time to launch a new brand of tea if it targets the growing health tea niche. Pukka, which sells organic and ethical teas, grew its sales by 15.4% in 2019, much faster than any of the top four brands, which shows there is strong demand for this type of tea. Health teas have a price elasticity of −0.5, so demand is price inelastic. This means a new brand could charge a premium price and a 10% price rise would only reduce sales volume by about 5%, so revenue would rise. The market research also supports this, as 62% of people say ethical sourcing is important when they buy tea, and 32% of women already drink health tea. As a result, a new brand that is ethical and focused on health tea could gain sales from customers who are not loyal to the traditional black tea brands. This shows that now is a good time to launch a new brand of tea, as long as it is aimed at the health tea niche.`,
 againstP:`However, the information suggests that now is not a good time to launch a new brand in the tea market as a whole. Sales of black tea fell from about 96 million kg in 2012 to about 68 million kg in 2020, a fall of almost 30%, and coffee is forecast to overtake tea by 2025. Young people are moving away from tea, with 28% of 18–24-year-olds never drinking black tea, so the market is likely to keep shrinking as older drinkers are replaced by younger ones. The top four brands also have a combined market share of 70.3% (£395m ÷ £562m), which gives them strong brand loyalty and acts as a barrier to entry (Porter's Five Forces). Black tea has a price elasticity of −1.2, so a new brand could not charge a premium and would struggle to compete on price against established brands with economies of scale. This shows that the tea market as a whole is a difficult one to enter at the moment.`,
 depends:['which segment the new brand targets (health/ethical niche or traditional black tea)','what its USP is and how it is positioned against the big four','the financial backing behind the brand to promote it and gain distribution','whether the trends shown continue'],
 report:'The examiner report says the best answers focused on the merits of launching "now", linked different parts of the data, and used their own calculations (such as the 70.32% market share from 1.2). Both yes and no conclusions were rewarded when supported.',
 model:`The information suggests that now is a good time to launch a new brand of tea only if it is a health or ethical tea, not a traditional black tea. The overall market is in decline, with black tea sales down almost 30% since 2012 and the top four brands holding 70.3% of sales, so a new black tea brand would be entering a shrinking market against strong competition. However, the health tea niche is moving in the opposite direction: Pukka grew by 15.4%, demand is price inelastic at −0.5, and 62% of buyers care about ethical sourcing, so a brand in this niche could charge a premium and win customers who are not loyal to the big four. So the decision depends mainly on the segment the brand targets. It also depends on whether the brand has enough financial backing to get into supermarkets against established names. Therefore, now is a good time to launch a health tea brand, but not a black tea brand.`},

{id:'P2-2023-01.4',type:'p2d',src:'Paper 2, June 2023, Q01.4',
 q:'With reference to the data provided, would you advise a potential investor to buy shares in AJS plc? Justify your view.',
 facts:['AJS plc makes non-recyclable packaging used by takeaway food outlets. It plans to invest £500 000 in new technology to increase capacity and become more capital intensive.','Appendix A: the investment has an NPV of £112 000 (operations manager\'s figures, 10% discount rate).','Appendix B: UK non-recyclable takeaway packaging sales fell from 1250 (2016) to 1050 (2022) thousand units a month.','Appendix C: UK recyclable takeaway packaging sales rose from 50 (2016) to 260 (2022) thousand units a month, including 175 to 260 in the last year.','Appendix D (AJS v industry): average salary £22 000 v £26 000; salary costs 40% v 35% of turnover; labour turnover 15% v 4%; days lost through industrial action 25 v 5; applicants per vacancy 14 v 55.','Appendix E: current assets £550 000; current liabilities £270 000; non-current liabilities £4m; operating profit £3m; gearing 22%; share price −15% over the year, compared with +8% for the top 100 UK companies. ROCE = 16.5%.'],
 forP:`On one hand, an investor could be advised to buy shares in AJS plc because its financial position is strong. Its ROCE is 16.5%, which means it makes 16.5p of operating profit for every £1 of capital employed, well above what an investor would earn from a savings account. Its current ratio is about 2.04 (£550 000 ÷ £270 000), so it has more than enough liquid assets to pay its short-term debts. Its gearing is low at 22%, so only a small proportion of its profit goes on interest, and it would not be badly affected if interest rates rose. The proposed investment also has a positive NPV of £112 000, which shows management is investing to grow and that the project should add value to the business. In addition, because the share price has fallen by 15% this year, the shares may be cheap compared with the profit the business makes, so there is a chance of a rise. This shows that, based on its finances, AJS could be a good investment.`,
 againstP:`However, an investor should be wary because AJS's market is in long-term decline. Sales of non-recyclable packaging fell from 1250 to 1050 thousand units a month between 2016 and 2022, a fall of 16%, while recyclable packaging grew from 50 to 260, more than five times its 2016 level, and rose by 49% in the last year alone. As takeaway outlets switch to recyclable packaging, AJS's sales are likely to keep falling, and the new investment would add capacity to a shrinking market. Investors already seem to see this, as AJS's share price fell by 15% while the top 100 companies rose by 8%, a gap of 23 percentage points. The HR data adds to the problem: labour turnover is 15% compared with 4% for the industry, 25 days were lost to industrial action compared with 5, and it gets only 14 applicants per vacancy compared with 55. This suggests poor motivation, which could reduce productivity and make it hard to adapt. This shows that AJS may be a poor investment for the future.`,
 depends:['whether the investor wants a long-term holding or a short-term gain','whether AJS moves into recyclable packaging','how reliable the forecasts are (the NPV figures come from the operations manager)','how much the investor can afford to lose'],
 report:'The examiner report says many students failed to focus on whether an investor should invest, and just gave arguments for and against. Better responses linked data together to build depth, and one-sided answers were a key limiting factor.',
 model:`I would advise a potential investor not to buy shares in AJS plc, because its strong finances are outweighed by the decline of its market. The ROCE of 16.5%, current ratio of 2.04 and low gearing of 22% show the business is healthy today, but these figures describe the past. What matters to an investor is future profit, and AJS's market fell by 16% while recyclable packaging grew more than fivefold, so its sales are likely to keep falling. The new investment makes this worse, as it adds capacity to a shrinking market. The 23 percentage point gap between AJS's share price and the top 100 suggests other investors have reached the same conclusion. However, the advice depends on whether AJS uses its strong finances to move into recyclable packaging, because then its low gearing would make it well placed to fund the change. Therefore, I would not advise buying shares unless AJS shows it plans to move into recyclable packaging.`},

{id:'P2-2024-01.4',type:'p2d',src:'Paper 2, June 2024, Q01.4',
 q:'In 2024, Biggs Ltd became the sponsor of a major televised sporting competition that attracts a wide range of ages and is popular with families. With reference to the information provided, evaluate whether Biggs Ltd\'s decision to promote its new product range in this way is a good idea.',
 facts:['Biggs Ltd is one of six firms that dominate the snack market, which has grown strongly for ten years, largely because of new snacks for children. Biggs\'s market share has been falling since 2019. Potato prices have risen 35% in two years.','Appendix A: revenue £47.569m (2023), £46.994m (2022); operating profit £4.650m (2023), £4.701m (2022). Gross profit margin fell from 48.6% to 46.9%.','Appendix B: labour productivity index 2023 — Biggs 100.3, industry average 105.9.','Appendix C: marketing spend as % of revenue in 2023 — Biggs 10.7%, average of the six largest firms 18.4%, market leader 24%. Biggs\'s 2024 forecast: 27.4%.','Appendix D (27 family interviews, 2021, scores out of 10): "I will definitely try the new products" — 6–16: 9.9, 17–35: 7.2, 36+: 1.8. "The campaign improves my view of the brand" — 8.3, 6.3, 8.0. "I frequently eat snacks at sports events" — 8.9, 6.9, 2.7.','Appendix E (3000 people, independent survey): share of snack purchases — 6–16: 19%, 17–35: 28%, 36+: 53%. Share of consumption — 46%, 43%, 11%.'],
 forP:`On one hand, sponsoring the sporting competition is a good idea because it targets the people who eat the most snacks. 6–16-year-olds consume 46% of all snacks, and in the research they scored 9.9 out of 10 for definitely trying the new products, as well as 8.9 for eating snacks at sports events. As the competition is popular with families, the sponsorship puts the new range in front of exactly this group, and children can then ask their parents to buy it. Parents also responded well, as the 36+ group scored 8.0 for the campaign improving their view of the brand. The sponsorship also fixes a clear weakness. In 2023 Biggs spent only 10.7% of its revenue on marketing, compared with an average of 18.4% for the six largest firms and 24% for the market leader, which helps to explain why its market share has been falling since 2019. This shows that the sponsorship is a good idea because it targets the main consumers of snacks and closes the gap in marketing spending.`,
 againstP:`However, the sponsorship may not be a good idea because of its cost and the reliability of the research. If revenue stays at about its 2023 level, increasing marketing to 27.4% of revenue means spending about £13m, compared with about £5.1m in 2023, an increase of roughly £8m. This is far more than Biggs's operating profit of £4.65m, at a time when its gross margin has fallen from 48.6% to 46.9% because of the 35% rise in potato prices. The people who actually buy the snacks, the over-36s, make 53% of purchases but scored only 1.8 for wanting to try the new products, so the campaign may not turn into sales. The research is also weak: it comes from only 27 families and was carried out in 2021, so it may not represent the market now. In contrast, the independent survey of 3000 people shows that the youngest group, who are the most enthusiastic, make only 19% of purchases. This shows that the sponsorship is a big financial risk that may not pay back.`,
 depends:['whether children\'s enthusiasm turns into purchases by parents, who make 53% of purchases','whether the roughly £8m extra spending can be afforded with operating profit of £4.65m','how reliable the 27-family research from 2021 is','competitors\' responses and the popularity of the competition itself'],
 report:'The mark scheme concludes there is a need for a new promotional strategy for the new product and this one seems targeted appropriately, but success may depend on competitor actions, the accuracy of the research, the popularity of the event and whether the reward justifies the considerable financial risk.',
 model:`Overall, sponsoring the competition is a good idea in principle, but the scale of the spending makes it a risky one. Biggs clearly needs to promote more, as it spent only 10.7% of revenue on marketing compared with 18.4% for the largest firms, and the event reaches 6–16-year-olds, who consume 46% of snacks and scored 9.9 for trying the range. The problem is that roughly £8m of extra spending is almost double last year's operating profit, so if the campaign fails Biggs could be pushed into a loss. Whether it works depends mainly on whether children's enthusiasm leads parents to buy, since the over-36s make 53% of purchases but scored just 1.8 for trying the products. It also depends on how far the 2021 research from only 27 families can be trusted. Therefore, the sponsorship is a good idea as a way to reach the right audience, but only if Biggs is confident that parents will follow their children's choices.`},

{id:'P2-2025-01.4',type:'p2d',src:'Paper 2, June 2025, Q01.4',
 q:'To what extent do the changes in the economic environment create more threats than opportunities for SCF? Use Appendix E and the other information provided in your answer.',
 facts:['SCF is a UK manufacturer of components sold to UK and EU computer manufacturers. Sales and profit margins have been falling. The new Chief Executive plans to replace the UK factory with a new factory in Spain to supply EU manufacturers directly. Some manufacturers will only buy components made in the EU.','Appendix B: operating profit £0.92m (2022), £0.71m (2023), £0.63m (2024). Non-current liabilities £2.8m and capital employed £10.2m in 2024 (gearing about 27%).','Appendix C (£m): EU market 452 (2022), 490 (2024), 552 (2026f), 601 (2028f). SCF\'s EU sales 16.1, 18.9, 23.1, 32.4. UK market 99, 102, 97, 96. SCF\'s UK sales 15.0, 16.1, 14.9, 13.7.','Appendix E (2025 → 2028): UK GDP growth 0.2%, 1.2%, 0.6%, 1.3%. EU GDP growth 3.1%, 3.6%, 4.2%, 5.1%. UK unemployment 5.5%, 6.2%, 7.1%, 7.0%. UK interest rates 5.4%, 5.2%, 5.5%, 6.1%. £1 = €1.18, €1.10, €1.03, €1.00.'],
 forP:`On one hand, the changes in the UK economy create threats for SCF. UK GDP growth is forecast to stay low, between 0.2% and 1.3%, while unemployment rises from 5.5% to 7.0% by 2028. This means fewer people in work and lower consumer confidence, so households will put off buying new computers. As a result, UK computer manufacturers will order fewer components from SCF, which is why SCF's UK sales are forecast to fall from £16.1m in 2024 to £13.7m in 2028, a fall of 15%. Interest rates are also forecast to rise from 5.4% to 6.1%, which makes buying computers on credit more expensive and reduces demand further. In addition, the pound is forecast to fall from €1.18 to €1.00. Since SCF plans to build its new factory in Spain and pay for it in euros, each euro will cost 18% more in pounds, which makes the move more expensive. This shows that the changes in the economic environment create clear threats for SCF.`,
 againstP:`However, the changes also create opportunities for SCF, especially in the EU. EU GDP growth is forecast to rise from 3.1% to 5.1%, so incomes in the EU will rise and more consumers and businesses will buy computers. This is why the EU market is forecast to grow from £490m in 2024 to £601m in 2028, and SCF's EU sales from £18.9m to £32.4m, an increase of 71%. The fall in the pound also helps SCF while it still makes components in the UK, as its prices become cheaper for EU buyers, making it more competitive against EU suppliers. In addition, SCF's gearing is only about 27% (£2.8m ÷ £10.2m), so rising interest rates will hurt it less than more highly geared rivals, who may cut back. By 2028 the EU is forecast to account for about 70% of SCF's sales (£32.4m ÷ £46.1m). This shows that the economic changes create a large opportunity for SCF in the EU.`,
 depends:['how successfully SCF carries out its plan to supply the EU from Spain','how much of SCF\'s business is in the EU compared with the UK (already 54% in 2024, about 70% by 2028)','how SCF responds to weak UK demand','the timescale of the forecasts'],
 report:'The examiner report says the best responses wrote a whole paragraph on threats and a whole paragraph on opportunities, chose data selectively, and used information from beyond Appendix E. Weaker answers worked through Appendix E line by line, described rising UK GDP as falling, or misread rising unemployment as fewer people available for work.',
 model:`Overall, the changes in the economic environment create more opportunities than threats for SCF, because its future lies in the EU rather than the UK. The threats are real: weak UK growth, rising unemployment and higher interest rates mean SCF's UK sales are forecast to fall by 15%. But the UK is already the smaller part of the business, and the opportunity in the EU is much bigger, with SCF's EU sales forecast to rise by 71% to £32.4m as EU growth reaches 5.1%. By 2028 around 70% of SCF's sales are expected to come from the EU, so what happens there matters far more than what happens in the UK. However, this depends on SCF carrying out its move to Spain successfully, since the weaker pound makes the factory more expensive and some EU buyers will only use EU-made components. Therefore, the economic changes create more opportunities than threats for SCF, as long as it can shift its business towards the EU.`},

/* ============================ PAPER 2, ESSAYS (16) ============================ */
{id:'P2-2018-02.3',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2018, Q02.3',
 q:'Lego\'s profits depend partly on exchange rate changes. To what extent do you think managers of all businesses need to worry about the impact of exchange rates on profits?',
 forP:`On one hand, managers need to worry about exchange rates when a large part of their costs or revenues is in foreign currency. This can be illustrated by UK supermarkets after the pound fell sharply following the vote to leave the EU in 2016. Much of the food and many of the products they sell are imported, so a weaker pound meant each item cost more in pounds. Suppliers passed these costs on, as when Unilever tried to raise its prices to Tesco by about 10%, leading Tesco to remove products such as Marmite from its website for a short time. As the UK grocery market is extremely competitive and customers are price sensitive, supermarkets could not raise their own prices by as much without losing customers to rivals such as Aldi and Lidl. As a result, their profit margins were squeezed. This shows that managers of businesses that rely on imports need to worry about the impact of exchange rates on profits.`,
 againstP:`However, managers of many businesses do not need to worry much about exchange rates. A local business such as a hairdresser or a builder buys most of its materials and labour in the UK and sells only to UK customers, so a change in the value of the pound has very little direct effect on its costs or prices. Even large international businesses can reduce the risk. Lego, for example, has factories in Mexico, Hungary and China, close to the markets they supply, so many of its costs are in the same currencies as its revenues. If a currency falls, both its costs and its revenues in that market fall together, so its profit margin is protected. Businesses can also use hedging, agreeing exchange rates in advance, so that they know what they will pay. This shows that managers of businesses that trade mainly in the UK, or that have protected themselves, do not need to worry much about exchange rates.`,
 depends:['how much of the business\'s costs and revenues are in foreign currency','how big the change is, in which direction and for how long','whether the business has hedged or produces in the same currency it sells in','how price elastic demand is, so whether higher costs can be passed on'],
 report:'The mark scheme says whether managers should worry depends on how much the business exports or imports, how large the change is and in which direction, against which currency and for how long, and whether it was anticipated. Strong responses ask what the answer depends on.',
 model:`Overall, not all managers need to worry about exchange rates, as it depends mainly on how much of the business's costs or revenues are in foreign currency. For UK supermarkets, which import much of what they sell, a fall in the pound raises costs that cannot easily be passed on to price-sensitive shoppers, so their margins are squeezed. For a local hairdresser, almost everything is bought and sold in pounds, so exchange rates barely affect profit. However, it also depends on whether the business has protected itself, since a company like Lego, which makes much of its product close to the markets it sells in, can trade internationally with far less risk. Therefore, managers of businesses that rely heavily on imports or exports need to worry about exchange rates, but for most businesses that trade mainly in the UK the impact on profit is small.`},

{id:'P2-2019-03.3',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2019, Q03.3',
 q:'Galin wants to increase labour productivity. To what extent do you think that attempts by businesses to increase labour productivity will be welcomed by employees?',
 forP:`On one hand, attempts to increase labour productivity can be welcomed by employees if they make work easier or bring them rewards. This can be illustrated by Microsoft Japan, which trialled a four-day working week in 2019 with no cut in pay and reported that productivity rose by about 40%. Employees had shorter meetings and more focused work, so they produced more per hour while gaining an extra day off. In Maslow's terms, this improved their work–life balance while keeping their pay secure. Where productivity rises because of better equipment, training or ways of working, employees can produce more without working harder. If the business shares the gains through higher pay or bonuses, their earnings rise, and a more productive business is more competitive, so their jobs are also more secure. This shows that attempts to increase labour productivity will be welcomed by employees when the gains are shared and the work does not become harder.`,
 againstP:`However, attempts to increase productivity will not be welcomed when they mean employees simply have to work harder. This can be seen in large online retail warehouses such as Amazon's, where pickers are set targets for the number of items they must pick each hour and their performance is closely monitored. Trade unions such as the GMB have complained that the pace of work is stressful and leads to injuries. Although output per worker rises, employees feel they are treated like machines, as in Taylor's scientific management, with little control over how they work. Productivity improvements can also lead to job losses, because if each employee produces more, the business needs fewer of them to make the same output. As a result, employees may resist the changes, motivation can fall and labour turnover can rise. This shows that attempts to increase labour productivity are unlikely to be welcomed if they increase the intensity of work or threaten jobs.`,
 depends:['how productivity is increased: better equipment, training and methods versus working harder','how the gains are shared: higher pay or bonuses, or just higher profit','whether jobs are at risk','whether employees are consulted and involved in the changes'],
 report:'The examiner report says students who did well stayed focused on whether employees would welcome the increase, and considered the nature of the work, the type of employee and the method used. Many drifted into how to increase productivity, or into motivation in general.',
 model:`On balance, attempts to increase labour productivity will only be welcomed by employees if they make the work better rather than harder, as it depends mainly on how the increase is achieved. At Microsoft Japan, productivity rose because work was organised more efficiently and employees gained a day off, so they welcomed it. In large warehouses, productivity rises because pickers are pushed to work faster against strict targets, so employees resent it. However, it also depends on how the gains are shared: even demanding changes may be accepted if employees receive higher pay or more job security in return. Therefore, attempts to increase labour productivity will be welcomed by employees when they come from better methods and the rewards are shared, but not when they simply increase the pace of work.`},

{id:'P2-2020N-02.4',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, November 2020, Q02.4',
 q:'The managers of a number of Nari\'s suppliers use Taylor\'s motivation theory when motivating their employees. To what extent do you think using Taylor\'s theory would be a good way for all businesses to motivate their employees?',
 forP:`On one hand, Taylor's theory can be a good way to motivate employees where the work is simple, repetitive and easy to measure. This can be illustrated by fruit picking on UK farms, where pickers are often paid piece rates for each kilo or punnet they pick. Taylor believed workers are motivated mainly by money, so linking pay directly to output gives them a reason to work faster. Because each picker's output can be measured easily, the farm only pays for what is produced, which keeps labour costs per unit under control. Many pickers are seasonal workers whose main aim is to earn as much as possible in a short time, so in Maslow's terms they are focused on physiological and safety needs, which money meets. As a result, productivity is high and the farm can get its crop picked quickly while it is fresh. This shows that Taylor's theory can be a good way to motivate employees when output is measurable and workers are motivated mainly by pay.`,
 againstP:`However, Taylor's theory would not be a good way to motivate employees in businesses where the work is creative, varied or based on teamwork. This can be seen at software companies such as Google, where developers solve complex problems and their output cannot be measured in units. Paying them per line of code would encourage poor-quality work rather than good solutions. These employees are well paid, so in Maslow's terms their lower needs are already met and they are motivated by esteem and self-actualisation, such as recognition and the chance to work on interesting projects. Herzberg would also say that pay is a hygiene factor: it can stop employees being dissatisfied but does not motivate them on its own. Treating skilled employees as if they only care about money could make them feel undervalued, so motivation would fall and talented staff might leave for rivals. This shows that Taylor's theory is not a good way to motivate employees in skilled, creative jobs.`,
 depends:['whether each worker\'s output can be measured and linked to pay','whether employees are motivated mainly by money (Maslow\'s lower needs) or by higher needs','whether quality matters more than quantity','the skill level and type of work'],
 report:'The mark scheme says the question asks whether Taylor\'s approach is a good way, not the best way, and that a response focusing on other theories without linking back to Taylor is not answering the question. The examiner report says many students described Taylor and other theories rather than judging whether it would work for all businesses.',
 model:`Overall, Taylor's theory would not be a good way for all businesses to motivate their employees, because it depends mainly on whether each worker's output can be measured and linked to pay. On a fruit farm, output is simple to measure and pickers are working mainly to earn money, so piece rates are an effective way to raise productivity. In a software business like Google, output cannot be counted in units and employees are motivated by recognition and interesting work, so paying by output would damage quality and motivation. It also depends on how important quality is, since paying for speed can lead to rushed work even in simple jobs. Therefore, Taylor's theory is a good way to motivate employees doing repetitive, measurable tasks, but not for businesses that rely on skilled or creative work.`},

{id:'P2-2022-03.6',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2022, Q03.6',
 q:'Reliability is the most important factor in a business\' choice of supplier. To what extent is this true for all businesses?',
 forP:`On one hand, reliability is the most important factor for businesses that use just-in-time production. This can be illustrated by car manufacturers such as Toyota, which hold very little buffer stock and rely on parts arriving exactly when they are needed on the production line. If a supplier delivers late, the line has to stop because there is nothing to fit, so the factory produces no cars while its fixed costs, such as wages and machinery, still have to be paid. As a result, unit costs rise sharply. Late deliveries to the factory also mean late deliveries to customers, which damages the manufacturer's reputation for reliability and could lose it future orders. Because one late part can stop the whole factory, a cheaper or slightly higher-quality supplier is not worth the risk if it is less dependable. This shows that reliability is the most important factor in choosing a supplier for businesses using just-in-time production.`,
 againstP:`However, reliability is not the most important factor for all businesses, as price or quality can matter more. For a business using cost leadership, such as Primark, the most important factor is the price charged by the supplier. Its customers buy mainly on price, so it needs the lowest possible cost of sales to offer low prices and still make a profit. It can deal with a less reliable supplier by ordering earlier or holding more stock, which costs less than paying higher prices. For a luxury business such as a high-end watchmaker, quality matters most, because customers pay a premium for perfection and one faulty part could damage a brand that has taken decades to build. In both cases, reliability is still useful, but it is not the main reason a supplier is chosen. This shows that reliability is not the most important factor in choosing a supplier for all businesses.`,
 depends:['how the business manages inventory: just-in-time versus holding buffer stock','its competitive strategy: cost leadership (price) or differentiation/luxury (quality)','what final customers expect','whether alternative suppliers are available'],
 report:'The examiner report says better answers related different circumstances where reliability is most important (such as JIT manufacturers) and where other factors matter more (price for cost leaders, quality for luxury producers), using exemplar businesses. Many answers raised lots of points without depth and struggled to reach a final evaluation.',
 model:`Overall, reliability is not the most important factor in choosing a supplier for all businesses, because it depends mainly on how the business manages its inventory. For a car manufacturer using just-in-time, one late delivery can stop the whole production line, so reliability matters more than anything else. For a cost leader like Primark, which can hold extra stock to cover late deliveries, the supplier's price has a bigger effect on profit, so price comes first. It also depends on the business's strategy, as a luxury watchmaker would put quality above both. Therefore, reliability is the most important factor for businesses that hold little stock, but for many other businesses price or quality is more important.`},

{id:'P2-2023-03.4',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2023, Q03.4',
 q:'\'Businesses will never act in a socially responsible way unless they are forced to do so by governments.\' To what extent is this true for all businesses?',
 forP:`On one hand, many businesses only act in a socially responsible way when the government forces them to. This can be illustrated by single-use plastic carrier bags. For years, supermarkets in England gave out billions of free plastic bags, even though they knew the bags harmed the environment. It was only when the government made large shops charge 5p per bag in 2015 that the number given out fell sharply, by more than 90% at the main supermarkets within a few years. This is because acting responsibly usually costs money, and if one supermarket had charged on its own, it risked losing customers to rivals that did not. In terms of Carroll's pyramid, most businesses meet their economic and legal responsibilities but go no further unless the law changes. This is the shareholder view, where the business's main duty is to make a profit within the law. This shows that many businesses will not act in a socially responsible way unless they are forced to by governments.`,
 againstP:`However, some businesses act in a socially responsible way without being forced, because it fits their values and their customers' expectations. This can be seen at Patagonia, the outdoor clothing business, which has given 1% of its sales to environmental groups since 1985 and repairs customers' old clothing, even though no law requires this. This puts it at the ethical and philanthropic levels of Carroll's pyramid. Patagonia's customers care strongly about the environment, so its responsible behaviour is a point of differentiation that builds brand loyalty and makes demand more price inelastic. As a result, acting responsibly helps it charge premium prices, so its profit motive and its social responsibility point in the same direction. This is the stakeholder view, where the business sees its duty to wider society as part of its success. This shows that businesses will act in a socially responsible way without government pressure when it benefits them and their customers.`,
 depends:['whether customers value social responsibility enough to reward it (target market)','ownership and culture: shareholder view versus stakeholder view','how public-facing the business is and the risk to its reputation','how costly acting responsibly is compared with rivals'],
 report:'The examiner report says strong responses gave a reasoned judgement on the circumstances in which the statement does and does not apply — for example depending on the business, its target market, ownership, culture, mission and how publicly facing it is — and used the wording of the statement.',
 model:`Overall, the statement is not true for all businesses, because it depends mainly on whether customers reward socially responsible behaviour. Where customers buy mainly on price, as with supermarket shoppers, acting responsibly is a cost that rivals may not match, so businesses like the supermarkets only cut plastic bags once the law forced every one of them to. Where customers care about the environment, as Patagonia's do, responsible behaviour increases loyalty and allows premium prices, so the business acts without any law. It also depends on the culture and owners of the business, as a business that takes the stakeholder view may act responsibly even when it costs it profit. Therefore, many businesses will only act responsibly when forced, but "never" is too strong, as businesses whose customers value it will act without government pressure.`},

{id:'P2-2025-02.4',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2025, Q02.4',
 q:'Seebus plc targets its marketing towards higher income customers. Should all businesses segment their markets based on income? Justify your view.',
 forP:`On one hand, segmenting by income is very useful for businesses whose products have a high income elasticity of demand. This can be illustrated by the car industry, where the Volkswagen Group sells Skoda models for buyers on lower and middle incomes, Volkswagen models for the middle of the market, and Audi and Porsche for higher earners. Because people on different incomes can afford very different prices, segmenting by income allows the group to design a product and price for each group of buyers. As a result, it can charge high prices to wealthier customers, who see a premium brand as a status symbol and so have price inelastic demand, while still selling large volumes to customers on lower incomes. It also helps the business target its promotion, for example advertising Porsche in places where high earners will see it. This shows that some businesses should segment their markets based on income, especially where income decides what customers can afford.`,
 againstP:`However, not all businesses should segment by income, because for many products other factors are better at predicting what people buy. This can be illustrated by Lego, which segments mainly by age, with Duplo for toddlers, standard sets for children and the Technic and Icons ranges for teenagers and adults. What a customer wants from Lego depends far more on their age than on their income, so segmenting by income would not help it design better products. For a local plumber, customers are segmented by where they live, because the plumber can only travel so far. For necessities such as bread or toothpaste, demand is income inelastic, so people on different incomes buy similar amounts and income tells the business little. As a result, segmenting by income would waste money on research and promotion. This shows that businesses should not all segment their markets based on income, as age, location or behaviour can be more useful.`,
 depends:['the income elasticity of demand for the product','what actually drives buying decisions in the market (income, age, location, behaviour)','how cheaply and accurately income data can be collected','whether income is combined with other bases (multi-segment approach)'],
 report:'The examiner report says many students suggested other types of segmentation and explained why these suited some businesses better than income. Some concluded that even businesses segmenting another way may still use income as a factor (a multi-segment approach). Weaker answers focused only on targeting higher income groups.',
 model:`Overall, not all businesses should segment their markets based on income, as it depends mainly on whether income is what decides what customers buy. For a car maker like Volkswagen, income decides which car a customer can afford, so income segmentation lets it sell to every part of the market at the right price. For Lego, what customers want depends far more on their age than on their income, so segmenting by age is more useful. It also depends on whether income is combined with other bases, because even Lego charges more for its adult sets, which are bought by people with more to spend. Therefore, businesses selling products with a high income elasticity of demand should segment by income, but for most others it should be one factor among several, not the main basis.`},

{id:'P2-2026-03.3',type:'p2o',src:'Paper 2, June 2026, Q03.3',
 q:'Would using performance-related pay be beneficial for all businesses? Justify your decision.',
 forP:`On one hand, performance-related pay can be beneficial for businesses where each employee's contribution can be measured clearly. This can be illustrated by estate agents, where staff are often paid a basic salary plus a bonus linked to the number and value of the houses they sell. Because the reward is linked to results, employees have a clear incentive to work harder to find buyers and agree sales. This links to Taylor's view that money motivates employees to increase their output. As a result, the agency sells more houses and earns more commission, while the bonus is only paid when revenue has actually been earned, so the cost is affordable. Performance-related pay can also help with recruitment and retention, because the most productive staff can earn more than they would elsewhere, so they are less likely to leave for a rival. This shows that performance-related pay can be beneficial for businesses where individual results can be measured and are closely linked to revenue.`,
 againstP:`However, performance-related pay is not beneficial for all businesses, because it can encourage the wrong behaviour when targets do not reflect what the business really values. This can be seen at Wells Fargo, the US bank, where staff were set demanding sales targets linked to their pay. To meet them, employees opened around two million accounts for customers without their permission. When this came out in 2016, the bank was fined hundreds of millions of dollars and its reputation was badly damaged. In jobs such as nursing, performance is hard to measure fairly and depends on teamwork, so linking pay to individual targets may cause resentment and reduce cooperation. Setting targets and carrying out appraisals also takes managers' time and adds cost. As a result, performance-related pay can lower quality, damage trust and harm the business. This shows that performance-related pay is not beneficial for businesses where performance is hard to measure or where targets can be met in harmful ways.`,
 depends:['whether individual performance can be measured fairly','whether the targets match what the business really wants (quality and customer trust, not just volume)','how much the work depends on teamwork','the cost of setting targets and running appraisals'],
 report:'The examiner report says many students treated PRP as piece-rate pay. Better responses considered circumstances in which PRP is more or less effective, with supported arguments and business examples. Evaluation was often what stopped answers reaching the top level.',
 model:`Overall, performance-related pay would not be beneficial for all businesses, because it depends mainly on whether individual performance can be measured fairly. For an estate agent, each employee's sales are easy to measure and directly create revenue, so linking pay to results raises effort and the bonus pays for itself. For a hospital ward, performance depends on teamwork and the quality of care, so individual targets are hard to set fairly and may damage cooperation. It also depends on whether the targets match what the business really wants, since Wells Fargo shows that rewarding volume alone can lead to behaviour that damages the business far more than it gains. Therefore, performance-related pay is beneficial for businesses where individual results are clear and closely linked to revenue, but not for those where quality and teamwork matter most.`},

/* ============================ PAPER 3, 24 MARKS (ALL OR ANY BUSINESSES) ============================ */
{id:'P3-2019-06',type:'p3o',src:'Paper 3, June 2019, Q06',
 q:'DWS Ltd is considering making a major strategic change. To what extent is a SWOT analysis valuable for any business deciding on its future strategy?',
 facts:['DWS Ltd makes high-quality metal tubing, adding significant value and charging relatively high prices. Its staff are highly committed (labour turnover 2% in 2008 against an industry average of 10%).','Since 2009 foreign rivals have improved their quality to close to DWS\'s level and charge lower prices. Mike\'s attempts to cut costs have had limited success.','Mike wants to produce only the highest value added, technically difficult items. Some directors want to move downmarket by cutting labour costs, customer service and some socially responsible activities.','The unhappy directors plan to present a SWOT analysis of DWS Ltd at the next board meeting.'],
 forP:`On one hand, a SWOT analysis is valuable when deciding on future strategy because it brings together the business's internal position and its external environment. This can be seen at DWS Ltd. A SWOT would show its strengths, such as its reputation for very high quality and a loyal, well-trained workforce with labour turnover of only 2% compared with an industry average of 10%. It would also show the threat from foreign rivals, who now offer similar quality at lower prices, and the weakness that DWS has struggled to cut its costs. Putting these together points towards Mike's strategy of focusing on the most technically difficult, high value added products, because this builds on DWS's strengths and avoids competing on price, where it is weak. As a result, the board can choose a strategy that fits the business rather than reacting to the latest problem. This shows that a SWOT analysis is valuable because it helps a business match its strategy to its strengths and the threats it faces.`,
 againstP:`However, a SWOT analysis has limits, because it does not make the decision. At DWS, the SWOT is being produced by the directors who want to move downmarket, so they may give more weight to the weaknesses, such as high costs, and less to the strengths, which shows how subjective it can be. Two groups can look at the same SWOT and reach opposite conclusions. A SWOT is also only a snapshot, so in fast-changing markets it can quickly go out of date. For example, BlackBerry knew in 2007 that Apple's new touchscreen iPhone was a threat, but it believed its strength in physical keyboards would keep customers loyal, and it lost most of its market share within a few years. Knowing the threat did not lead it to the right strategy. Other tools, such as Porter's Five Forces or Ansoff's Matrix, are needed to analyse the market and the options in more depth. This shows that a SWOT analysis has limited value on its own when deciding on future strategy.`,
 depends:['who carries it out and how objectively (bias)','how quickly the market changes (snapshot)','whether it is used with other tools such as Porter\'s Five Forces and Ansoff\'s Matrix','how well managers act on what it shows'],
 report:'The examiner report says many students wrote descriptively about the benefits and drawbacks of SWOT. The most effective answers evaluated its usefulness in the process of deciding strategy, and on "any business" questions a response illustrated only by the case business can still reach all but the very highest level.',
 model:`Overall, a SWOT analysis is valuable for any business deciding on its future strategy, but only as a starting point, because it shows the position the business is in rather than telling it what to do. For DWS, a SWOT makes it clear that its strengths are quality and a loyal workforce while its threat is cheaper foreign rivals, which points strongly towards the high value added strategy. But BlackBerry shows that knowing the threats is not enough, as the business still has to interpret them correctly and act quickly. How valuable a SWOT is therefore depends mainly on how objectively it is carried out, since the DWS directors may use theirs to support the decision they already want. It also depends on whether it is used alongside tools such as Porter's Five Forces and Ansoff's Matrix. Therefore, a SWOT analysis is valuable in helping any business understand its position, but its value in choosing a strategy depends on how honestly it is produced and how well managers act on it.`},

{id:'P3-2020N-06',type:'p3o',src:'Paper 3, November 2020, Q06',
 q:'To what extent does a commitment to Corporate Social Responsibility decrease profit for all businesses?',
 facts:['Sunport PLC is a multinational selling household cleaning, personal care and food products in nearly 200 markets.','Its former Chief Executive committed it to a socially responsible approach, including suppliers who share its values and an aim to use only recyclable packaging. The share price rose consistently.','Appendix A: sales £48bn (2016), £51bn, £52bn, £54bn (2019); operating profit £4.6bn, £4.5bn, £4.8bn, £5.1bn. Average operating profit margin of major rivals in 2019: 12%.','The new Chief Executive wants every division to reach a 12% operating profit margin, partly by changing to cheaper suppliers and cutting spending on environmental initiatives.','Appendix B: in Europe, recyclable packaging is food shoppers\' second most important issue, after taste.'],
 forP:`On one hand, a commitment to CSR can decrease profit because it raises costs. This can be seen at Sunport PLC. Under Martin, it chose suppliers who shared its values and aimed to use only recyclable packaging, both of which cost more than the cheapest alternatives. As a result, its operating profit margin in 2019 was only 9.4% (£5.1bn ÷ £54bn), compared with an average of 12% for its major rivals. If Sunport had matched its rivals' margin, its operating profit would have been about £6.5bn, about £1.4bn higher. This is why Elaine plans to switch to cheaper suppliers and cut environmental spending. The effect is likely to be greater for a business whose customers buy mainly on price, such as a budget supermarket, because it cannot pass the extra costs of CSR on without losing customers to rivals. This shows that a commitment to CSR can decrease profit, because it adds costs that may not be recovered through higher prices.`,
 againstP:`However, a commitment to CSR does not decrease profit for all businesses, because it can increase demand and reduce some costs. At Sunport, recyclable packaging is the second most important issue for food shoppers in Europe, after taste, so its commitment helps to attract and keep customers there. Its loyal suppliers have also given it reliable deliveries and worked with it on new products, which reduces the costs of disruption. This may be why its share price rose consistently, as investors expected strong long-term profit. Outside the case, the cosmetics retailer Lush campaigns against animal testing and sells many products with no packaging, and its customers are willing to pay premium prices for products that match their values. This makes demand more price inelastic, so the extra costs of CSR are more than covered by higher prices and loyalty. This shows that a commitment to CSR can maintain or even increase profit when customers value it.`,
 depends:['whether customers value CSR enough to pay more or stay loyal (target market, B2C v B2B)','how much CSR adds to costs in that business','whether rivals already act responsibly (CSR may protect rather than raise profit)','the time period: short-term costs versus long-term loyalty and reputation'],
 report:'The examiner report says the bulk of responses should explore scenarios in which commitment to CSR will and will not decrease profit. Many introduced scenarios where failing to commit to CSR decreased profit, which does not answer the question. Top answers addressed "all businesses" by showing in what contexts profit would or would not fall.',
 model:`Overall, a commitment to CSR does not decrease profit for all businesses, because it depends mainly on whether customers value it enough to pay for it. For a business selling on price, such as a budget supermarket, CSR is mainly an extra cost that cannot be passed on, so profit falls. For Lush, customers buy partly because of its values, so CSR supports premium prices and protects profit. Sunport sits between the two: its margin of 9.4% against rivals' 12% suggests CSR has cost it profit in the short term, but in Europe customers rank recyclable packaging second only to taste, so cutting it could lose sales there. It also depends on the time period, as the costs come first while the loyalty and reputation build over time, which may explain why Sunport's share price kept rising. Therefore, CSR is likely to decrease profit in the short term for businesses whose customers buy on price, but not for businesses whose customers value it.`},

{id:'P3-2025-06',type:'p3o',src:'Paper 3, June 2025, Q06',
 q:'Effective capacity management involves ensuring a business has the right level of capacity in the right place to match demand. To what extent is effective capacity management always harder for large, multinational businesses than small businesses?',
 facts:['VBM is a European car manufacturer whose success was based on diesel engines. Low emission zones and bans on new petrol and diesel cars (UK 2030, EU 2035) have caused diesel sales to fall rapidly in Europe.','Demand for its diesel vehicles is rising in parts of South-East Asia, particularly Vietnam.','VBM is closing three of its four European factories, with around 5000 redundancies, and building a new factory in Vietnam. The network diagram shows it could open in a year, but the HR Director says recruitment will take one month longer and training two months longer than planned, as VBM has no experience of operating in Vietnam.'],
 forP:`On one hand, capacity management can be harder for large multinationals because the decisions are bigger and more complex. This can be seen at VBM. Demand has moved between continents, falling in Europe because of diesel bans while rising in Vietnam, so VBM has to reduce capacity in one region and build it in another at the same time. Closing three of its four European factories involves around 5000 redundancies, which means consulting with stakeholders, paying redundancy settlements and risking bad publicity. Building a factory in Vietnam means dealing with different legislation and recruitment systems in a country where VBM has no experience, and the HR Director already expects the project to take three months longer than planned. While capacity is being moved, VBM may have too much capacity in Europe and too little in Vietnam, so it loses sales and pays for unused factories at the same time. This shows that capacity management can be harder for a large multinational because it has to match capacity to demand in many places at once.`,
 againstP:`However, capacity management is not always harder for large multinationals, because they have resources that small businesses lack. A large business can move production to factories with spare capacity in other countries, and a fall in demand in one region may be balanced by growth in another, as VBM's rising sales in Vietnam partly offset falling sales in Europe. Multinationals can also afford better forecasting and can add capacity quickly when demand peaks. For example, Amazon recruits thousands of temporary staff in the UK each year to cope with the Christmas rush. In contrast, a small seasonal business such as a family-run hotel in Cornwall has fixed capacity: it cannot add rooms in August or move them elsewhere in winter, so it is full in summer and has empty rooms for much of the year. As one big booking or one cancelled event is a large share of its demand, changes hit it harder. This shows that capacity management can be just as hard, or harder, for small businesses with fewer resources.`,
 depends:['the resources available to adjust capacity (finance, forecasting, flexible staff)','how far demand changes and in how many locations','the type of product: easy to move or flex (digital, transport) or fixed (buildings, factories)','how flexible and experienced the managers are, regardless of size'],
 report:'The examiner report says many students explained why capacity management is hard for multinationals, but fewer explained why it may be as hard or harder for small firms. Inaccurate assertions, such as small businesses all being start-ups, undermined arguments. One response was criticised for introducing new issues in the conclusion that had not been developed earlier.',
 model:`Effective capacity management is not always harder for large multinationals, because it depends mainly on the resources a business has compared with the size of the change it faces. VBM shows that when demand moves between continents, capacity management becomes very complex, with factory closures in Europe and a new factory in a country where it has no experience. But multinationals also have the finance and flexibility to respond, such as Amazon taking on thousands of temporary staff each Christmas, which a small hotel with a fixed number of rooms cannot do. So large businesses usually face bigger and more complex capacity decisions, while small businesses have less ability to respond to even small changes in demand. It also depends on the type of product, since capacity that can be moved or hired is far easier to manage than buildings and factories. Therefore, capacity management is harder for multinationals when demand shifts across countries, as at VBM, but not always, as small businesses with fixed capacity often find it harder to match demand.`},

/* ============================ PAPER 3, 20 MARKS (CASE DATA) ============================ */
{id:'P3-2017-05',type:'p3d',src:'Paper 3, June 2017, Q05',
 q:'Most journalists blamed the fall in KMH plc\'s profits on either a failure to control costs or a slowdown in the growth of the global economy. Which of these two reasons do you believe is the main cause? Justify your answer using information from the case study and Table 2.',
 facts:['KMH plc designs, builds and maintains marine engines for cruise ships and luxury yachts. Luxury yacht buyers include very wealthy executives from Russia and Middle Eastern oil-producing countries.','Global growth has been low, incomes in many rich countries have barely risen in real terms, demand for cruise holidays has been affected, and oil prices have fallen.','Table 2 (£m): revenue 7756.5 (2014), 7441.7 (2015), 6862.5 (2016). Gross profit 1658.4, 1562.6, 1399.8. Operating expenses 903.5, 906.5, 910.4. Operating profit 754.9, 656.1, 489.4.','The new Chief Executive plans to cut 140 jobs, mostly senior managers (one in three), saving around £12m a year.'],
 forP:`On one hand, the slowdown in the global economy is the main cause of the fall in KMH's profits, because it caused a large fall in revenue. Revenue fell from £7756.5m in 2014 to £6862.5m in 2016, a fall of £894m or 11.5%. As incomes in rich countries have barely risen, demand for cruise holidays has weakened, so cruise operators are delaying orders for new ships and engines. Falling oil prices have also reduced the wealth of KMH's luxury yacht customers in Russia and the Middle East, and have reduced the need for its fuel-efficient engines. If KMH had kept its 2014 gross profit margin of 21.4%, the fall in revenue alone would have reduced gross profit by about £191m (£894m × 21.4%). Since operating profit fell by £265.5m in total, the fall in revenue explains about 72% of it. This shows that the global economic slowdown is the main cause of the fall in KMH's profits.`,
 againstP:`However, a failure to control costs also played a part in the fall in profits. Cost of sales fell by only 10.4%, less than the 11.5% fall in revenue, so the gross profit margin fell from 21.4% to 20.4%. This one percentage point fall cost KMH about £67.5m of gross profit in 2016. At the same time, operating expenses rose from £903.5m to £910.4m, an increase of £6.9m, while revenue was falling. A business facing falling sales should cut its overheads, but KMH allowed them to rise. Together, these cost problems account for about £74m, or 28%, of the £265.5m fall in operating profit. Sir Rodney's decision to cut one in three senior managers to save £12m a year also suggests that the new Chief Executive believed costs had not been controlled properly. This shows that a failure to control costs made the fall in KMH's profits worse.`,
 depends:['the relative size of the two effects (about £191m from falling revenue against about £74m from costs)','whether the global economy recovers, given oil prices are not expected to rise for a couple of years','whether the cost cuts are achieved'],
 report:'The examiner report says the very best responses quantified the changes in revenue and costs, noting the far greater size of the fall in revenue, and students should not be afraid of making a clear judgement that one factor is more important than another. Less successful responses failed to make or justify a judgement.',
 model:`The slowdown in the global economy is the main cause of the fall in KMH's profits, because its effect is far larger than the effect of poor cost control. The £894m fall in revenue, caused by weaker demand for cruise holidays and falling oil prices hitting yacht buyers, accounts for about £191m of the £265.5m fall in operating profit, roughly 72%. The failure to control costs did make things worse, with the lower gross margin and rising operating expenses costing about £74m, but even if costs had been perfectly controlled, profit would still have fallen by around £191m. However, how long the problem lasts depends on the economy, since oil prices are not expected to rise for a couple of years, so cutting costs is the only part KMH can control in the meantime. Therefore, the global slowdown is the main cause, with weak cost control a secondary one that made the fall bigger.`},

{id:'P3-2023-05',type:'p3d',src:'Paper 3, June 2023, Q05',
 q:'Logger Boards Ltd has been offered a bank loan at 5% interest per year for the £500 000 needed to finance the building of its new production facility. Using Appendix B and other information, to what extent is using a bank loan too big a risk for the business? Use quantitative and qualitative information to justify your view.',
 facts:['Logger Boards Ltd makes professional-quality surfboards from recycled materials and dominates its niche. Owner Bodhi Doyle has always aimed to minimise risk in his decisions and has turned down chances to expand by takeover.','Demand is at a record high and expected to keep growing. The new facility would raise capacity from 2500 to 7500 boards a year; forecast output in the first year is 3000. Fixed costs would rise from £125 000 to £250 000.','The bank has offered a long-term loan of £500 000 at 5% a year.','Appendix B (£000s): current assets 246; current liabilities 120; non-current liabilities 400; total equity 1000. Sales turnover 1125; cost of sales 750; expenses 125; finance cost 20; profit for the year 230.'],
 forP:`On one hand, the loan could be too big a risk because it greatly increases Logger Boards' debt. Its gearing would rise from 28.6% (£400k ÷ £1400k) to 47.4% (£900k ÷ £1900k), close to 50%, so nearly half of its capital would be borrowed. Interest would rise by £25 000 a year (5% of £500 000), more than doubling its finance costs from £20 000 to £45 000, and these must be paid whatever happens to sales. At the same time, fixed costs will double from £125 000 to £250 000, but in the first year the facility will only produce 3000 boards from a capacity of 7500, a capacity utilisation of just 40%. If the expected growth in demand does not happen, Logger Boards will have higher fixed costs and interest to cover from a similar level of sales. This is especially risky for a business whose owner has always tried to minimise risk, and which has succeeded for 25 years by avoiding big gambles. This shows that the bank loan could be too big a risk for Logger Boards.`,
 againstP:`However, the loan is unlikely to be too big a risk, because Logger Boards is in a strong financial position to cope with it. Its operating profit is £250 000 (£1125k − £750k − £125k), so even with the extra interest, it would cover its total interest of £45 000 about 5.6 times. Its current ratio is 2.05 (£246k ÷ £120k), so it has plenty of liquid assets to meet its short-term debts and the loan repayments. Gearing of 47.4% is still below 50%, so it would not be classed as highly geared. The interest rate of 5% is fairly low. Demand has grown for years without any spending on promotion and is at a record high, so there is good reason to expect sales to rise. If the business sells 3000 boards at its current average price of £450 (£1 125 000 ÷ 2500), revenue would rise to about £1.35m. This shows that the loan is not too big a risk for Logger Boards.`,
 depends:['whether demand grows as expected so the new capacity is used','Bodhi\'s attitude to risk','how far interest cover and liquidity can absorb a fall in sales'],
 report:'The examiner report says responses that kept a tight focus on the level of risk in taking out the loan scored well, especially when judgements were focused on risk and used calculated gearing and current ratios. Weaker responses drifted into whether a loan was a good source of finance or whether the business should expand. A Level 2 example was let down by a conclusion based on flawed analysis that answered a slightly different question.',
 model:`On balance, using a bank loan is not too big a risk for Logger Boards, although it does increase the risk a lot for a business that has always avoided it. The key figures are its ability to pay: operating profit of £250 000 would cover total interest of £45 000 more than five times, and a current ratio of 2.05 means it can meet its short-term debts. Gearing rising to 47.4% is a big change, but it stays below 50% and the 5% interest rate is low. The main risk is that the new capacity is only 40% used at first while fixed costs double, so the level of risk depends mainly on demand continuing to grow as forecast. As demand has grown for years without any promotion, this seems likely. Therefore, the loan is a manageable risk rather than too big a risk, but it will only feel acceptable to Bodhi if he is prepared to take on more risk than he has in the past.`},

{id:'P3-2026-05',type:'p3d',src:'Paper 3, June 2026, Q05',
 q:'Managing cash flow is more important than making profit for Danni\'s business. To what extent do you agree? Use information from Appendix C and Appendix D within your answer.',
 facts:['Danni runs a building business, now a private limited company, usually working on three or four jobs at once. Customers may pay in advance, monthly, or only when work is finished.','He has two skilled employees paid weekly. He took out a loan after an accident two years ago and has borrowed to buy equipment. His suppliers are mostly large UK wholesalers.','Appendix B: gearing 15% (2 years ago), 50%, 60% (this year).','Appendix C (£, weeks 1–4): cash from customers 3000, 22 000, 0, 5000. Payments to employees 1000, 1000, 1200, 900. Payments to suppliers 12 000, 1000, 4000, 1000. Other expenses 1700, 300, 300, 1000. Loan interest and repayments 0, 0, 0, 3600. Net cash flow −11 700, +19 700, −5500, −1500.','Appendix D (£, 2 years ago, 1 year ago, this year): turnover 360 000, 280 000, 340 000. Cost of sales 230 400, 165 200, 207 400. Expenses 57 600, 67 200, 68 000.'],
 forP:`On one hand, managing cash flow is more important for Danni at the moment because his cash inflows are very irregular. Appendix C shows net cash flow swinging from −£11 700 in week 1 to +£19 700 in week 2, then −£5500 and −£1500, because customers pay at different points in each job. Over the four weeks his net cash flow is only £1000. However, his outflows are regular and cannot be delayed: his two employees must be paid every week, and loan interest and repayments of £3600 are due in week 4, which could be over £40 000 a year if paid monthly. With gearing rising from 15% to 60%, more of his cash is committed to the bank. Because his suppliers are large wholesalers, he is unlikely to be able to negotiate longer credit when cash is short. If he cannot pay his employees on time, the two skilled workers he depends on may leave. This shows that managing cash flow is more important for Danni's business, because a cash shortage in a bad week could stop the business even if it is profitable.`,
 againstP:`However, making a profit is just as important, because without profit Danni's business cannot survive in the long run. Appendix D shows his gross profit is £132 600 this year (£340 000 − £207 400), a gross margin of 39%, which is healthy and higher than the 36% of two years ago. But his expenses have risen from £57 600 to £68 000 over the two years, an increase of 18%, while turnover has fallen from £360 000 to £340 000. As a result, his gross profit minus expenses has fallen from £72 000 two years ago to £64 600 this year. Profit is what pays Danni's own income and what will eventually allow him to repay his loans. If profit keeps falling, the business will not generate enough cash over time however carefully he times his payments. This shows that making a profit is at least as important as managing cash flow for Danni's business.`,
 depends:['the time period: cash flow decides short-term survival, profit decides long-term survival','how well Danni can time customer payments (deposits and monthly payments) against weekly wages and loan payments','whether rising expenses and debt keep eating into profit'],
 report:'The examiner report says few students picked up the sporadic nature of Danni\'s cash inflows alongside the positive net cash flow over the four weeks. The best evaluation recognised that neither cash flow nor profit represented an existential crisis for Danni\'s business, and avoided exaggerated claims. Using precise terminology and keeping the difference between cash and profit clear mattered.',
 model:`Overall, I agree that managing cash flow is more important for Danni's business at the moment, although neither cash nor profit is in crisis. His business is profitable, with gross profit of £132 600 and a margin of 39%, so profit is not the immediate problem. The bigger risk is timing: his net cash flow swings from −£11 700 to +£19 700 from one week to the next, while wages are due every week and £3600 of loan payments fell due in a single week, with gearing now at 60%. A single bad week could leave him unable to pay his skilled employees, even though the four weeks together show a positive net cash flow of £1000. However, this depends on the time period, as rising expenses have cut his gross profit minus expenses from £72 000 to £64 600, and if that continues, profit will become the bigger problem. Therefore, managing cash flow is more important in the short term, but Danni must also stop his expenses rising to protect his profit in the long term.`}
];

const byId=Object.fromEntries(Q.map(x=>[x.id,x]));
window.CBANK={TYPES,Q,byId};
})();
